    "claim": "Rapamycin (sirolimus) and its analog everolimus function as mTOR inhibitors.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "rationale": "The provided context explicitly identifies everolimus as an mTOR inhibitor and mentions sirolimus as a pharmacologic inhibitor of the mTOR pathway."
  },
  {
    "claim": "The mTOR-signaling pathway is a central regulator of immune cell activation and fibroproliferative responses in organ transplantation.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "rationale": "The second abstract states that mTOR is a central regulator of immune cell differentiation and fibroproliferative responses in transplantation."
  },
  {
    "claim": "These agents suppress the phosphorylation of downstream proteins such as p70 ribosomal S6 kinase and 4E-binding protein 1 (4EBP1).",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "rationale": "The first abstract confirms that everolimus significantly suppressed p-4EBP1 phosphorylation and assessed p70 ribosomal S6 kinase."
  },
  {
    "claim": "These agents attenuate renal interstitial fibrosis and limit structural cell proliferation.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "rationale": "The first abstract states everolimus attenuates renal interstitial fibrosis, and the second abstract states mTOR inhibitors limit structural cell proliferation."
  },
  {
    "claim": "mTOR inhibition serves as a therapeutic strategy to manage immune-mediated injury and reduce the progression of chronic allograft dysfunction in renal transplant recipients.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "rationale": "The first abstract links mTOR inhibition to the reduction of interstitial fibrosis (a driver of chronic allograft dysfunction), and the second abstract discusses mTOR inhibition as a strategy to manage immune-mediated injury."
  }
]
